{
  "_comment": [
    "Regression baseline for the real-tower fixture at --seed 7, checked by",
    "Tools/RecoveryEvaluation/check_regression.py. These are NOT release",
    "gates: the sensor model is still invented (ADR 0014), so the absolute",
    "numbers mean little. What they catch is movement -- a change that makes",
    "the solver or verifier worse than it is today.",
    "",
    "false_complete_rate is the one that matters most and is currently",
    "perfect. Nothing else in CI protects it.",
    "",
    "convergence_rate is 0.0 because the reconstructed edge model injects a",
    "systematic per-view bias the solver cannot overcome. It is baselined at",
    "its true value rather than aspirationally, so the entry starts guarding",
    "the moment calibration lifts it.",
    "",
    "Tolerances absorb small floating-point differences in the Metal raster",
    "pass across runner GPUs. They are not headroom for regressions.",
    "Regenerate with --update and explain the movement in the commit message.",
    "",
    "Count metrics (2026-09-25) guard the corpus shape under the rates:",
    "`exact` for how many cases each metric covers, `lower_is_better` for",
    "failure counts and for expected-complete rows the generator dropped",
    "below strong detectability. A rate that improves because its cases",
    "vanished is a regression, not a win.",
    "",
    "Lattice guards (2026-10-07, iOS 27 Phase 4), added by hand: no tower",
    "pose is depth-ambiguous (exact 0); every solve still reports",
    "ambiguous, because the reconstructed sensor's per-view bias keeps the",
    "margin near 1.0; and pitch_off counts poses that settled within 2 mm",
    "of a whole stud pitch at true yaw. Under that bias such landings are",
    "mostly chance, so these guard movement, not aliasing evidence."
  ],
